WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.786
Redirects can change where a request goes after the client has already
started an operation.

2
00:00:04.786 --> 00:00:10.677
Some redirects are expected, such as a service moving from one endpoint to
another within the same trust boundary.

3
00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:14.174
Others may cross to a different host, scheme, port, or network zone.

4
00:00:14.174 --> 00:00:22.580
A secure HTTP client should define whether redirects are allowed, which
destinations are acceptable, and how many redirects are reasonable for the
integration.

5
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.569
Sensitive headers and credentials need special handling across redirects.

6
00:00:26.569 --> 00:00:32.705
A token intended for one service should not be sent to a different
destination merely because a response pointed there.

7
00:00:32.705 --> 00:00:37.491
Depending on the client and configuration, headers may be preserved,
stripped, or changed.

8
00:00:37.491 --> 00:00:44.854
Teams should understand the behavior of their HTTP client and avoid
assuming that automatic redirect handling is safe for every integration.

9
00:00:44.854 --> 00:00:47.615
Proxy configuration can also be security-relevant.

10
00:00:47.615 --> 00:00:59.458
Corporate proxies, service meshes, API gateways, environment-driven proxy
variables, and deployment routing can affect where requests travel, who
can observe metadata, which certificates are used, and how failures
appear.

11
00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:06.637
A Python application should know whether proxy behavior is part of the
intended architecture or accidental inherited environment state.

12
00:01:06.637 --> 00:01:10.564
Proxy settings can be helpful, but they should be visible enough for
review.

13
00:01:10.564 --> 00:01:16.025
Connection reuse, cookies, and cross-environment behavior deserve
attention in non-browser clients.

14
00:01:16.025 --> 00:01:22.345
Connection pooling can improve performance, but credentials, destinations,
and TLS expectations should remain clear.

15
00:01:22.345 --> 00:01:26.886
Cookies may appear in some HTTP client workflows even though the client is
not a browser.

16
00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:32.224
A staging configuration should not accidentally send production tokens
through a development proxy.

17
00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:38.360
Cross-boundary behavior should be reviewed whenever requests move between
environments, services, or trust zones.
